WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.090
Film Courage: How did you get a screenwriting
agent right after college was it?

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.620
Todd Berger, filmmaker and screenwriter: I
went to school at the University of Texas

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:10.830
and they used to do this thing called The
Hollywood Showcase where they would take an

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:17.470
hour’s worth of student films and they would
show it out here in LA at the DGA (Director’s

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:23.439
Guild of America) and the would invite alumni
(alumni?) of the university just to come and

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:27.419
see the short films, just like “What are
the kids doing back at school?”

00:00:27.419 --> 00:00:35.289
And so the year I graduated I had written
a short film that played and I had written

00:00:35.289 --> 00:00:41.340
and directed one (so I had two) a puppet short
that became the inspiration for THE HAPPYTIME

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.240
MURDERS.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.059
So you know I was living in Austin.

00:00:45.059 --> 00:00:46.160
I was delivering pizza.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:52.649
I was going to move to Chicago and do Second
City and go to New York because I grew up

00:00:52.649 --> 00:00:53.769
thinking “LA?

00:00:53.769 --> 00:00:54.769
Hollywood?

00:00:54.769 --> 00:00:55.769
Sell out?

00:00:55.769 --> 00:01:01.679
What am I going to sell out?”

00:01:01.679 --> 00:01:07.440
And so they picked two of my short films for
this thing and I graduated like on Saturday

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.810
and a couple of days later I came to LA just
to go the screening, just because I’d never

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:15.140
been to LA before and I just graduated and
I was like “I’ll go to the Hollywood Walk

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:16.140
of Fame.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.370
I’ll go to the beach.”

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:23.750
And so I went to the screening and there is
an agent at the screening who just happened

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:29.001
to go to my college and he was just there
to see the shorts and he liked my short film

00:01:29.001 --> 00:01:30.001
so much.

00:01:30.001 --> 00:01:35.590
And he walked up to me after the reception
afterwards and said “I loved your short

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:36.590
film.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:40.020
Have you written any features before because
I’m a literary agent and I represent writers?”

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.780
And I said “Well, I wrote one for class.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.130
I took a screenwriting class.”

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:47.610
And he’s like “Well send it to me, I’d
love to read it.”

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.810
So I got his card and I went back to Austin
and I mailed it to him.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:56.240
This was back in the day when people mailed
things.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.750
And I just kept delivering pizza and saving
up money to move to Chicago and a couple months

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.280
later he called me and was like “Hey, we
love your script.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.990
We read it and we want to sign you and represent
you as a writer.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.520
But you have to move to Los Angeles.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.790
You can’t move to Chicago because we wouldn’t
be able to send you to meetings.”

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:12.790
And I was like “Ok!”

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:16.930
Because I was out delivering pizza and I went
back and I’m like “I’m out!

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:18.970
I’m moving to Hollywood.”

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:26.030
And just from these short films that I’d
done, I’d gotten someone’s attention.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:31.720
And I know the story is ridiculous and I was
plucked from obscurity but also part of the

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:37.000
story is I had these short films that somebody
saw and he came to me and said what else do

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.281
you have and I was able to say “Well, I
have this script that I wrote” to him and

00:02:41.281 --> 00:02:45.450
he read it and he liked it.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:53.900
And so I have a lot of friends he make short
films as a calling card which is an amazing

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:58.030
thing to get people’s attention.

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:02.520
And I always tell them “Well, you have to
be prepared for if someone sees you short

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.560
film and likes it and then they say “What
else do you have?

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:07.760
Do you have any features?

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.130
Do you have something?”

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:12.360
You have to be able to say “Yes, now here
is the other thing.”

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:16.250
Because I have a lot of friends who have this
short that they work very hard on and they’ll

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:21.330
go play a festival and they’ll meet an agent
or a producer and the agent will say “What

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:22.330
else do you have?”

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:27.569
And they’ll say “Well…I’ve got some
ideas of stuff I want to try and start working

00:03:27.569 --> 00:03:30.960
on…but…uh…nothing right now.”

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:31.960
Like you can’t do that.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:34.860
You have to have something ready to go, that’s
my piece of advice.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:39.040
Film Courage: And…going back to when you
were delivering pizzas did you actually get

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.390
the call when you were on a run?

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:49.840
Todd: So this is actually a really funny story…so
I graduated, I was living in Austin.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:56.150
I was moving out of my apartment because I
was going to spend 6 months living with a

00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:02.340
friend of mine in her spare bedroom saving
up money into the summer…I graduated in

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:06.080
May when you graduate and then I was going
to move in January so I had like 7 months

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.489
where I was just going to deliver pizza and
make money.

00:04:08.489 --> 00:04:13.930
And so I had to move out of my apartment,
so I moved everything out of my apartment…except…my

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:15.430
answering machine.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:20.600
The only thing that was still in my apartment
(I don’t even thing I had a cell phone yet.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.310
This is 2001.

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:29.509
So I had an answering machine and when I gave
that agent a business card (by the way, he’s

00:04:29.509 --> 00:04:30.509
still my agent).

00:04:30.509 --> 00:04:31.690
He’s still my agent to this day.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:35.099
Here we are 16 years later.

00:04:35.099 --> 00:04:42.180
When I gave him my card in Los Angeles the
only number that was on it was my apartment

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:45.690
in Austin because I didn’t have a cell phone.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:53.650
The only way he had of contacting me was at
my apartment and I was like well I guess he

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:58.270
never got my script or didn’t read my script
so the only thing left in my apartment was

00:04:58.270 --> 00:04:59.659
my answering machine.

00:04:59.659 --> 00:05:04.770
So I went and moved everything over to the
new place and I had to go back and clean and

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:08.389
take the answering machine away.

00:05:08.389 --> 00:05:13.599
So I go back to clean and there is a red light
blinking on the answering machine and I was

00:05:13.599 --> 00:05:15.020
like “Oh, I have a message?”

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:19.160
And BEEP…”Hey, Todd, Steven from the Agency.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:20.469
We read your script.

00:05:20.469 --> 00:05:22.500
Give me a call.”

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:26.189
Had I gotten to my apartment like an hour
earlier and unplugged the machine, I’d just

00:05:26.189 --> 00:05:27.870
never have gotten the call from him.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:33.379
Who knows if he would have tried to track
me down by some other means.

00:05:33.379 --> 00:05:35.919
But that message was waiting for me.

00:05:35.919 --> 00:05:40.409
And the only thing left for me in that apartment
was my answering machine so I’m knocking

00:05:40.409 --> 00:05:43.599
on wood because I’m really lucky.

00:05:43.599 --> 00:05:50.930
But I then got a cell phone and I stayed in
Austin for like three months delivering pizzas

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:53.669
because I still needed to save up money to
move to LA because just because I had an agent

00:05:53.669 --> 00:05:58.389
didn’t mean I was rich or anything, I still
needed to afford to move to LA.

00:05:58.389 --> 00:06:00.400
So I was delivering pizzas and saving up money.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:08.279
I bought a cell phone and I gave my agent
my new cell phone number and he took my script

00:06:08.279 --> 00:06:14.680
that I wrote for class and he gave me some
notes on it and I did a new draft and then

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:19.529
he sent it around town as a spec script and
he showed it to a bunch of producers.

00:06:19.529 --> 00:06:25.389
And like I didn’t know what that meant and
like I’m out on a delivery in Austin and

00:06:25.389 --> 00:06:30.639
my phone rings and it’s my Hollywood agent
and he’s like “Todd, hey!

00:06:30.639 --> 00:06:35.939
We went out with your script and we just set
it up at Paramount (it was Kelsey Grammer’s

00:06:35.939 --> 00:06:38.669
company at Paramount) and I was like “Oh?

00:06:38.669 --> 00:06:40.150
Oh, cool.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:41.150
I don’t know what that means?

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:42.150
What does that mean?”

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:45.500
And he’s like “Well, it’s ten thousand
dollar option plus you’re going to get a

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:49.550
Writer’s Guild minimum of $27,000 to do
the guaranteed rewrite.”

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:50.550
And I’m like “I’m sorry, what?

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:51.550
What?”

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:56.850
And he’s like “Yeah, so it’s a guaranteed
$37,000 dollars.”

00:06:56.850 --> 00:07:03.830
And I was like “That’s more money than
I’ve heard of or imagined.”

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:06.249
Because I’m making like $7.00 an hour at
this pizza place.

00:07:06.249 --> 00:07:10.569
So it was like a dream, I couldn’t believe
it.

00:07:10.569 --> 00:07:14.312
And I went back to the pizza place where I
worked and I was like “I’m out!

00:07:14.312 --> 00:07:15.580
I quit!

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:18.050
I’m Hollywood now.”

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:19.610
But then I’m a nice guy so I actually finished
my shift.

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:20.610
Film Courage: Good.

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:21.610
That’s admirable.

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:27.309
Todd: I think I actually finished working
one more shift and but then I quit and moved

00:07:27.309 --> 00:07:28.689
to LA.

00:07:28.689 --> 00:07:35.630
But…that being said…I did learn the harsh
realities of…I then…it’s not like everything

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:38.909
I wrote got sold or I got jobs that easy.

00:07:38.909 --> 00:07:43.849
It’s like I thought that’s how it worked
because my first script right out the gate

00:07:43.849 --> 00:07:46.419
got optioned.

00:07:46.419 --> 00:07:50.919
So I was like “Oh, every script I ever write
now is going to get optioned and set up but

00:07:50.919 --> 00:07:54.029
no it took awhile for it to happen again.

00:07:54.029 --> 00:07:57.800
I hit lightning in a bottle the first time.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.499
And I moved out to LA thinking “I’m a
wonder kin.

00:08:00.499 --> 00:08:02.960
I’m an awesome, hotshot 22-year-old.”

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:09.899
Film Courage: How soon after?

00:08:09.899 --> 00:08:12.059
Todd: Oh, pretty soon.

00:08:12.059 --> 00:08:14.800
Pretty soon, like I wrote another script.

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.270
You know I moved out and I wrote another script
and I’m like “Cool, let’s show this

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:18.270
one.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:19.270
I’m sure I’m going to set this one up.”

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:20.270
And no one cared.

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:21.520
People were like “Nah…no.

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:22.520
What else?”

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:24.110
And I was like “Oh?”

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:30.240
And then years…years went by before I set
up one of my own scripts because I learned

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:35.440
very quickly that when you want to be a working
writer, it’s not like you write scripts

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:36.970
and then sell them to people.

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:43.639
Most jobs you ever work are you write scripts
for other people based on their ideas or pre-existing

00:08:43.639 --> 00:08:44.639
material.

00:08:44.639 --> 00:08:48.850
So it’s not like people are looking for
original scripts as much as it’s “Hey,

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:54.360
we need a writer to rewrite that draft of
the NINJA TURTLES movie.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:09:01.310
Or we bought the IP for some eighties cartoon
and we need a writer to write a script for

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:03.120
it.”

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:09.199
So I very quickly realized “Oh, I actually
have to work very hard now to go to meetings

00:09:09.199 --> 00:09:16.310
and meet producers and try to get an assignment”
is what it’s called in order to get paid

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:19.100
to write stuff.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:20.730
And for years that all I did.

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:26.180
I mean to this day it’s my primary source
of income is that I just do assignment work

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:27.540
which is a hard job.

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:32.160
Like you spend half your time trying to get
the next assignment and then the little free

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:36.150
time you do have you try to work on an original
script and you hopefully can sell it to someone

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:36.160
some day.

